<title>BELGIUM: GIB swings back to profit in 96/97.</title>
<headline>GIB swings back to profit in 96/97.</headline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS 1997-03-27</dateline>
<text>
<p>Year to Jan 31, 1997</p>
<p>(in billion Belgian francs unless stated)</p>
<p>    Group net profit (ex-minorities)	3.39 vs   4.39 loss</p>
<p>    Current profit				2.37 vs   2.09</p>
<p>    Extraordinary gain			  0.84 vs   5.53 loss</p>
<p>    Consolidated turnover		   216.56 vs 227.78</p>
<p>    Like-for-like turnover		  214.55 vs 210.71</p>
<p>    Cash flow				     9.15 vs   8.60</p>
<p>    Operating profit			    3.07 vs   3.16</p>
<p>NOTE - GIB is Belgium's second largest retail group after Delhaize in terms of turnover.</p>
<p>GIB said its 1996/97 figures were provisional. The 1995/96 results included charges linked to the sale of loss-making U.S. unit Handy Andy.</p>
<p>In a statement, GIB said it planned to invest nearly seven billion Belgian francs in its 1997/98 financial year.</p>
<p>GIB said in 1996/97 its flagship GB supermarket unit maintained market share for the second year. GB sales rose 2.2 percent, and sales of food products rose 3.3 percent. GB has achieved a 22 percent improvement in productivity in its stores since 1994, GIB said.</p>
<p>GB forms part of GIB's supermarkets and hypermarkets division, which accounts for 67 percent of total group sales.</p>
<p>-- Brussels Newsroom +32 2 287 6810, Fax +32 2 230 7710</p>
